Search PATH for /bin/name when no such file exists.
A command of the form /bin/name, /usr/bin/name, /sbin/name or
/usr/sbin/name runs the file if there is one, and otherwise the applet
or builtin of that name. For any other name find_command() searches
PATH, so "command -v /sbin/name" reports the program found there, and
the applets (env, xargs, find -exec, ...) run it, but shellexec()
rewrote argv[0] to the bare name and then skipped the PATH search
because the original name had a path. The shell failed with "not found"
when running a command it had just located.

Search PATH in shellexec() too, unless find_command() picked the file
itself (index -1). "exec /bin/name", which does not go through
find_command(), searches the whole PATH as the applets do.

---
 shell/ash.c |    4 ++++
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+)

--- a/shell/ash.c
+++ b/shell/ash.c
@@ -7313,6 +7313,10 @@
 			}
 			if (!find_builtin(name)) {
 				argv[0] = (char *)name;
+				/* Search PATH for the name, unless
+				 * find_command() chose the file itself */
+				if (idx >= 0)
+					path_fail = FALSE;
 			}
 		}
 		if (!path_fail) {
